# Exploring Practical AI Tools

## 1. Purpose of This Exploration

Artificial intelligence tools can support technical learning, research, programming, and problem-solving. During this activity, the focus is on understanding how different AI assistants can be used effectively, how prompts influence their responses, and why important information must be verified before relying on it.

## 2. Tools and Their Potential Uses

| AI Tool                                      | Main Capabilities                                                                       | Possible Engineering Application                                       |
| -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| [ChatGPT](https://chatgpt.com/)              | Concept explanations, coding assistance, structured responses, and interactive learning | Understanding Verilog concepts or debugging a Python script            |
| [Google Gemini](https://gemini.google.com/)  | Information exploration, explanations, and learning support                             | Exploring introductory machine learning topics                         |
| [Claude](https://claude.ai/)                 | Document analysis, detailed explanations, and programming support                       | Reviewing a script and understanding its logic                         |
| [NotebookLM](https://notebooklm.google.com/) | Answering questions using supplied sources and helping locate relevant passages         | Studying ML notes and checking explanations against reference material |
| [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/)     | Web research and discovering relevant references                                        | Finding official documentation for open-source engineering tools       |

These tools have overlapping capabilities, but their usefulness depends on the task, the available features, and the quality of the information provided. Their responses should not be treated as equally reliable for every technical question.

## 3. Experimenting with Prompt Design

A prompt describes the task an AI tool should perform. Including the subject, the learner's current level, relevant constraints, and the expected response format can make the output more useful.

### Experiment A — Making a Question More Specific

**Initial prompt:**

> Explain machine learning.

**Improved prompt:**

> Explain supervised learning to an electronics engineering student who is new to AI. Use a simple semiconductor-related example, explain the difference between features and labels, and finish with three questions to test my understanding.

**Observation to record:**

The revised prompt provides a clearer learning objective and asks for a particular example and output structure. After testing both prompts, compare their explanations for clarity, relevance, and technical accuracy.

### Experiment B — Learning a Technical Concept

**Initial prompt:**

> Explain OpenROAD.

**Improved prompt:**

> Describe the purpose of OpenROAD in an open-source ASIC physical-design flow. Explain its main role, typical inputs and outputs, and how it relates to floorplanning, placement, clock-tree synthesis, routing, and timing analysis. Use beginner-friendly language and identify any details that should be checked in the official documentation.

**Observation to record:**

The second prompt narrows the discussion to physical design and identifies the information needed. Verify the tool's actual capabilities and workflow against its documentation instead of assuming that every requested feature is supported in the same way.

### Experiment C — Requesting a Structured Answer

**Initial prompt:**

> Help me learn Python.

**Improved prompt:**

> Prepare a short Python practice exercise for a beginner. Include the problem statement, a small example input and output, hints, and test cases. Do not reveal the complete solution until I have attempted the problem.

**Observation to record:**

The revised prompt encourages active practice instead of simply receiving an answer. Evaluate whether the exercise matches your current CS50P progress and whether you can explain your solution independently.

## 4. Comparing AI Explanations

A useful way to evaluate AI assistants is to give two tools the same technical question and compare their answers against a trusted reference.

**Question selected:**

> What is the difference between classification and regression in machine learning?

| Comparison Point   | What to Examine                                                 |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Definition         | Does each tool explain the difference correctly?                |
| Examples           | Are the examples appropriate for each type of task?             |
| Clarity            | Which response is easier for a beginner to understand?          |
| Technical accuracy | Are there unsupported statements or misleading simplifications? |
| Verification       | Can the main claims be confirmed using reliable documentation?  |

**My comparison:**
After running the same prompt through two AI tools, record which explanation was more understandable, what differences you noticed, and whether either response contained an error or missing detail.

## 5. Checking an AI-Generated Claim

AI-generated answers may sound convincing even when a statement is incomplete or incorrect. Checking important claims against primary sources is therefore an essential part of technical learning.

**Claim selected for verification:**

> Classification models predict categories, whereas regression models estimate numerical values.

**Reference used:**

Google's Machine Learning Crash Course provides introductory material on classification and related evaluation concepts.

Reference: [Google ML Crash Course — Classification](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification)

**Verification process:**

1. Read the relevant section of the reference.
2. Compare its explanation with the AI-generated answer.
3. Check whether the examples match the definitions.
4. Note any differences or unsupported statements.

**Conclusion to record:**
Write whether the reference supports the claim and briefly explain what you learned. Make sure the conclusion reflects the material you actually reviewed.

## 6. Using AI as a Learning Assistant

AI tools are most useful when they help develop understanding rather than replace independent thinking. They can explain unfamiliar concepts, suggest practice problems, identify possible coding mistakes, and organise research questions.

For engineering tasks, generated answers and code still require careful review. For example, an AI-generated script related to a physical-design flow should be checked against the installed tool version, its documentation, and the intended input files before execution.

AI services should not receive confidential RTL, proprietary design files, restricted EDA documentation, or other sensitive engineering material unless the applicable policies explicitly permit it.

## 7. Key Takeaways

* Clear prompts help direct an AI tool toward a specific learning objective.
* Context and output requirements can make answers more relevant.
* Comparing multiple explanations can reveal differences in clarity and detail.
* Official documentation provides an important basis for checking technical claims.
* AI-generated code must be understood and tested before use.
* The learner remains responsible for the accuracy of the final work.

## 8. Further Practice

To continue improving my use of AI tools, I will practise asking focused technical questions, compare responses where useful, and verify important statements against reliable references. I will also use AI-generated exercises to strengthen my Python knowledge and explore engineering concepts without relying entirely on generated answers.
